## Why Asked
Tests understanding of graph traversal fundamentals and algorithm selection
## Key Concepts
Queue vs stack implementation, time complexity O(V+E), space complexity differences, use cases
## Code Example
```
// BFS
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    if (!visited.has(node)) {
      visited.add(node);
      queue.push(...graph[node]);
    }
  }
}

// DFS
function dfs(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (!visited.has(node)) {
      visited.add(node);
      stack.push(...graph[node]);
    }
  }
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
How to implement recursively? What about cycle detection? Time vs space trade-offs?

## Approach

This problem combines bit manipulation with in-place partitioning:

### Step 1: Find the Unique Element
Use XOR operation on all elements. Since `a ^ a = 0` and `a ^ 0 = a`, all paired elements cancel out, leaving only the unique element.

```javascript
let unique = 0;
for (let num of arr) {
  unique ^= num;
}
```

### Step 2: Partition Around Unique Element
Use three-way partitioning (similar to Dutch National Flag):
- Elements less than unique go left
- Unique element in middle
- Elements greater than unique go right

```javascript
let low = 0, mid = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
while (mid <= high) {
  if (arr[mid] < unique) {
    [arr[low], arr[mid]] = [arr[mid], arr[low]];
    low++; mid++;
  } else if (arr[mid] > unique) {
    [arr[mid], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[mid]];
    high--;
  } else {
    mid++;
  }
}
```

### Step 3: Sort Pairs
Within each partition, pairs are already together. Use counting sort or simply swap pairs into position.

**Time Complexity:** O(n)
**Space Complexity:** O(1)

## Why Asked
Tests understanding of fundamental sorting algorithms and their trade-offs in real-world scenarios.
## Key Concepts
- Divide and conquer strategies
- Partitioning vs merging
- In-place vs auxiliary space
- Stability and performance characteristics
## Code Example
```
// Quicksort partition
function partition(arr, low, high) {
  const pivot = arr[high];
  let i = low - 1;
  for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  [arr[i + 1], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[i + 1]];
  return i + 1;
}

// Mergesort merge
function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) result.push(left[i++]);
    else result.push(right[j++]);
  }
  return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
- When would you choose one over the other?
- How does pivot selection affect quicksort performance?
- Can you implement a stable quicksort?

## Why Asked
Tests tree traversal understanding and space optimization at DE Shaw
## Key Concepts
BST property, inorder traversal, O(1) space
## Code Example
```
int kthSmallest(TreeNode* root, int k) {
    int count = 0;
    TreeNode* curr = root;
    while (curr) {
        if (!curr->left) {
            if (++count == k) return curr->val;
            curr = curr->right;
        } else {
            TreeNode* pred = curr->left;
            while (pred->right && pred->right != curr)
                pred = pred->right;
            if (!pred->right) {
                pred->right = curr;
                curr = curr->left;
            } else {
                pred->right = null;
                if (++count == k) return curr->val;
                curr = curr->right;
            }
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
Find kth largest, handle duplicates, optimize for multiple queries
